(project tuna-gate-receipts
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: gate receipts - tuna's first product customer is the delivery gate. A gate result (a build, a test suite, a lint) becomes a claim whose verdict is a PRIM-FREE tuna run over an attested observation record, so any reviewer identity can re-derive the verdict from content-addressed artifacts instead of trusting a sentence in a commit message. Research question, stated so it can be refuted: does a replayable verdict over attested observations catch fail-open gates, and is it cheap enough that the factory and its reviewers actually use it?")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-23 design discussion (roerick + Claude, onboarding session). MOTIVATION, both observed the same day: (1) tuna's three-engine differential gate built no binaries after the monorepo merge, failed 93/93, and the runtest rule's `&& ... || echo` reported it as "sbcl missing: skipped" - make -C tuna test exited 0 (fixed in 3cd448d5); (2) pp's image/test/data_spaces_test.ml prints SKIP and marks every case [OK] when Postgres is unreachable, so a default-env run reports 15/15 green while testing nothing. The organization policy installed the same day (nomia: engineering.verify-change, engineering.independent-review) already demands gate evidence over claims; nomia's review-change check-evidence.sh says of itself "a completeness check, not a truth check". This chapter is the truth half. FRAMING DECISION: tuna does not run the gate. Shell commands are host effects that no journal can make replayable; what tuna CAN make replayable is the DECISION (pass, fail, unverified) given the facts, and what it can make tamper-evident is WHO asserted the facts. Everything below follows from keeping those two apart.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-24 G1 LANDED. Artifacts: scripts/gate-receipts/verdict.sabra (prim-free, self-contained), scripts/gate-receipts/adapt.sh (alcotest, differential, make-test), scripts/gate-receipts/encode.sh (the record codec in awk), scripts/gate-receipt.sh (the wrapper), tests/gate_receipt_tests.ml (19 alcotest cases, no PG, no HTTP). claims.sh gained the gate claim kind for the 8-segment receipt path. EVIDENCE: fixtures green; before/after replay at a90487b3 fail (the log's 93/93 FAILED outranks its misleading sbcl-missing line) and at 3cd448d5 pass; a non-admin second identity verified the receipt while getting 404 on the runner's run row; every observation field changes the input hash, and a tampered citation naming changed facts under the original result reports MISMATCH. DEVIATIONS FROM THE ORIGINAL TEXT, forced by reality: (a) the chapter predicted unverified for the a90487b3 log, but that log states 93/93 FAILED, so the honest verdict is fail; (b) "changing any field breaks verification of the original claim" is not a shape claims.sh has - it verifies a citation, and the original citation still pins its own immutable input - so the measured property is the hash binding plus a mismatch when a citation names changed facts under the original result. Project status stays planned because the G2 verify line (check-evidence receipt mode) is unrun; the three G1 subsections are marked implemented.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-27 G2 AND G3 IMPLEMENTED (factory executions brg_39322cdf, branch factory/spec-tuna-borg-gate-receipts-g-1127-brg_39, merged 35e4a932, and brg_d4031dc1, branch factory/spec-tuna-borg-gate-receipts-g-1127-brg_d4, merged 0624350c; independent factory code review PASS on both). G2 CODE: nomia/skills/review-change/scripts/check-evidence.sh gains --receipts, which resolves each command's receipt with claims.sh verify and fails on a MISMATCH, an unresolvable claim, a missing verdict line, a fail receipt against exit 0, or a reproduced unverified receipt against a claimed pass; a command without a receipt is unchanged. claims.sh verify prints verdict=<pass|fail|unverified> only for a kind=gate claim, mapped from the reproduced result tree (10/110/1110). G3 CODE: the implement and merge TEST gates run under tuna/scripts/gate-receipt.sh through an injected-or-real receipt runner (factory/loop.py receipt_gates, gate_receipt, receipt_env; factory/evidence.py receipt_decision), record {label, cmd, rc, receipt, verdict} per gate, and block a pass whose test-gate receipt is not pass. OWNER DECISION (2026-11-27, after G3 landed): only the TEST gate is a receipt-law gate. A successful build prints no test summary, so its receipt is unverified by construction (measured 2026-11-27: dune build exit 0 -> counts unparsed -> unverified); a red build still blocks on its own exit, an unmintable build runs directly and the record says so, and a gate that does not apply is recorded not-run rather than as a missing receipt. The revision landed in 11f030f3 (e80deacb, f56760a7, bcd5b0ba). ADAPTERS landed 56b3d3e0: ANSI CSI stripping, unittest shapes, COUNTS aggregation, unexpected successes counted as failures, inconsistent COUNTS treated as unparsed; tests/gate_receipt_tests.ml 22/22 green on its branch. EVIDENCE: sh nomia/skills/review-change/tests/check-evidence-test.sh prints COUNTS run=14 passed=14 failed=0 and PASS; a live mint at 35e4a932 through the real wrapper and runner identity prints counts=14 14 0 0 and verdict=pass, and claims.sh verify on that claim prints VERIFIED plus verdict=pass, exit 0 - G2's verify line, run after G2 landed. G3 is proven end to end by G2's own landing: the merge log records build receipt verdict=unverified rc=0 (advanced; build gates are not law) and test receipt verdict=pass rc=0 receipt verified, then MERGED 12f5ef60 -> master 35e4a932, pushed to origin/main. factory/tests/test_merge_gates.py and factory/tests/test_evidence.py: 38 tests OK, six consecutive runs after fixing a record-selection flake in the merge test (it picked the newest change set by file-name order and by head sha; two tests can build the same commit in the same second, so it now selects by card id). OBSERVED FAILURE, FIXED: the receipt slug carried the full 69-character brg_ card id, which pushed the minted audit projection to 14747 ternary digits and the store's PUT surface refused it (wrapper exit 2), blocking every merge; the slug now keeps the first 16 characters (b35067a2) and a test pins it. UNRUN: the full dune runtest was not re-run for this book-only close-out; the adapter suite ran green on its branch.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-27 ADAPTERS EXTENDED (operator repair, independent review). The factory's own gates are not all alcotest: `python3 -m unittest` and shell harnesses had no adapter, and a successful `dune build` has no counts at all. Measured: a green alcotest run under the wrapper parsed `counts=unparsed` while its captured log carried colour escape bytes; `python3 -m unittest` and a PASS-printing shell script were unparsed too. The strip is defensive: a pty-captured or FORCE_COLORed log must not turn a green suite into unverified. adapt.sh now strips CSI escapes, understands the unittest shapes and an explicit COUNTS line in the aggregate, counts "unexpected successes" as failures, refuses an inconsistent COUNTS line, and the wrapper accepts --adapter unittest. tests/gate_receipt_tests.ml pins the colour case, the unittest shapes (including the real 3.14 unexpected-successes log), the aggregate path the factory actually uses, and COUNTS consistency; 22 cases green. THE BUILD-GATE LIMIT IS NOT AN ADAPTER GAP: a successful build prints no test summary, so a build receipt is unverified by construction. The owner decided (2026-11-27) that only the TEST gate is a receipt-law gate; a build's own exit still blocks, and when the wrapper cannot mint the build runs directly and the record says so. That decision lives in the G3 subsection.|) *)
 (section gate-receipts
  (doc "Three layers with different trust: OBSERVATION (attested by a runner identity, not proven), VERDICT (a pure tuna program, re-derivable by anyone), REPRODUCTION (optional re-execution of the command at the same revision by a reviewer).")
  (status implemented)
  (subsection observation-record
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      A runner (the factory loop, an agent's verify-change pass, or a
      human's wrapper script) executes one check and records an
      observation. It is a tuna record (dialect v1.1: a list of
      [key value] two-lists) passed to the verdict run as an INPUT,
      never fetched by a prim, so the verdict run stays prim-free:

        gate        check slug, e.g. tuna/runtest
        project     owning project dir, e.g. tuna
        command     the exact argv string run
        rev         git commit the tree was at (40 hex)
        tree_clean  1 when the working tree matched rev, else 0
        diff_hash   sha256 of `git diff HEAD` when tree_clean is 0
        exit        process exit status
        log_hash    sha256 of combined stdout+stderr bytes; the bytes
                    are stored as a byte value (byte-values.borg) and
                    resolvable by hash
        counts      [run passed failed skipped], parsed from the log by
                    the runner's harness adapter, or the atom unparsed
        adapter     name+version of the parser that produced counts
        runner      identity that executed the command
        started_at  UTC timestamp (attribution only; not a verdict input)

      Secrets never enter the record: env is recorded by NAME only, and
      the log is the runner's responsibility to redact before hashing
      (a redacted log is a different log hash, stated, never silent).

      Parsing logs in the calculus is deliberately out of scope for v1:
      strings are bit lists, so a 10k-line test log is a priced
      non-starter. The adapter runs host-side and its counts are
      attested facts like the exit code. The log hash lets a reviewer
      re-parse. This is the prim-creep discipline from the substrate
      discussion: no new prim is admitted to make the verdict "smarter"
      unless a refuted fixture shows the pure policy cannot decide it.

      ENCODING, concrete (G1).  A record is canonical: entries sorted by
      ascending key, keys are the dialect's small-nat tags (Stem^n Leaf),
      and each value uses the book's one codec:

        1 gate        cstr string (the slug as invoked)
        2 project     cstr string
        3 command     cstr string (bash %q of the argv)
        4 rev         40-hex cstr string
        5 tree_clean  law-5 int, 0 or 1
        6 diff_hash   cstr string; the empty string is the leaf
        7 exit        law-5 int
        8 log_hash    64-hex cstr string
        9 counts      [run passed failed skipped] of law-5 ints, or the
                      atom %10 (Stem Leaf) for unparsed
       10 adapter     cstr string, e.g. adapt.sh/make-test/v1
       11 runner      cstr string (the identity name)
       12 started_at  cstr string, UTC ISO-8601

      A receipt's VERDICT is one tag, never a string: %10 pass, %110
      fail, %1110 unverified.  unverified is first-class and never
      folded into pass.  The skip-allowed list is a bracket list of cstr
      gate slugs (the empty list is the leaf).  Both inputs are stored
      as tree values before the run, so claims.sh can cite them by hash.

      ADAPTERS (host-side, scripts/gate-receipts/adapt.sh, bash+awk):
        alcotest     "Test Successful in Xs. N tests run." is N passes;
                     "F failures! ... N tests run." adds F failures
                     (run = N+F); every line printed starting SKIP adds
                     a skip even when alcotest says [OK].
        differential "all N corpus entries agree" is N passes;
                     "F/N corpus entries FAILED" is N run, N-F passed,
                     F failed.  A FAILED line outranks the misleading
                     "sbcl missing: ... skipped" sentence: the old
                     and-or fallback rule printed that sentence after a
                     real failure, and believing it is the fail-open
                     this chapter exists to catch.
        make-test    sum every recognised suite summary in the gate log.
                     A skipped suite (postgres down, node absent, sbcl
                     missing) with no failure makes the gate unparsed:
                     part of the gate did not run.  A real failure still
                     outranks it, because the log states it.  Extra
                     checks in the same log that print their own lines
                     (the filmstrip CDP gate) are not counted; their
                     failure rides the command's exit status.  The
                     aggregate also reads the unittest shapes and an
                     explicit COUNTS line, so a log mixing harnesses
                     sums them as one gate.
        unittest     python -m unittest: "Ran N tests in ..." is the run
                     count; "OK" makes them passes and "OK (skipped=K)"
                     adds K skips; "FAILED (...)" takes failures, errors
                     and unexpected successes.  A FAILED line with no
                     recognised count is one failure, never zero - stock
                     3.14 emits "FAILED (unexpected successes=N)" for a
                     suite that did not do what it said.
        COUNTS line  (make-test only) a harness with no standard summary
                     prints one line
                     "COUNTS run=N passed=N failed=N skipped=N" and it is
                     a suite summary like any other; an inconsistent line
                     (run != passed + failed + skipped) is not a summary.
                     The line is authored by the harness whose exit code
                     and log hash the receipt attests, exactly as the
                     alcotest summary is.

      ANSI escape sequences are stripped before matching: a colourised
      summary is still a summary, and a pty-captured log must not make a
      green suite unparsed.  A token containing a CSI sequence cannot
      forge a summary, because the match is anchored on the fixed prefix.
      A log with no recognisable suite summary is unparsed.  A cached
      dune run that prints nothing is therefore unverified, never pass;
      run the gate from a clean build (a fresh worktree or --force).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection verdict-program
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The verdict is a pure sabra program over one observation record,
      compiled once and addressed by hash. v1 policy, in order:

        exit is nonzero                                   -> fail
        counts is unparsed                                -> unverified
        run is 0                                          -> unverified
        failed is nonzero                                 -> fail
        skipped is nonzero and gate not in skip-allowed   -> unverified
        tree_clean is 0 and diff_hash is empty            -> unverified
        otherwise                                         -> pass

      unverified is a first-class answer, never folded into pass. It is
      the organization rule "an unrun or unavailable check is
      unverified, never a pass" made executable. The skip-allowed set
      is a program INPUT (a list of gate slugs), so a project widening
      it is a visible change to the cited inputs, not a code edit.

      The pp PG suite's SKIP lines parse to counts [15 15 0 15], so
      that gate lands as unverified.  The 2026-11-23 differential log
      does NOT: it printed "differential: 93/93 corpus entries FAILED"
      next to the rule's misleading "sbcl missing: ... skipped"
      sentence, so an honest adapter must report fail.  Believing the
      skip sentence would reproduce the fail-open the chapter exists to
      catch.  The bare "sbcl missing" shape, with no FAILED summary,
      is counts [0 0 0 0] and therefore unverified.  The adapter must
      also count a printed SKIP as a skip even when the harness reports
      [OK]; that rule and the FAILED-over-skip rule are the only pieces
      of gate-specific knowledge the system needs.

      LANDED (G1).  scripts/gate-receipts/verdict.sabra is the program.
      It is self-contained: the frozen stdlib v1 helper definitions
      (if, tree-case, list-fold, tree-eq, rec-get, ...) are copied
      inline, so POST /api/programs {"source": ...} compiles it with no
      caller dictionary.  It is prim-free: the fixture suite evaluates
      every case with a host that records prim calls and asserts zero.
      The fixture matrix pins all seven policy lines, including exit
      nonzero outranking unparsed counts and a skip-allowed gate still
      failing on a failed test.  Counts are compared as law-5 ints to
      the canonical zero %200, never by leaf/stem shape.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection minting-and-verifying
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The runner POSTs the verdict run (program = verdict hash,
      inputs = [observation, skip-allowed]) and mints it with the
      existing claims machinery (scripts/claims.sh mint), at

        u.<runner>.claim.<date>.gate.<project>.<rev12>.<gate-slug>

      LANDED (G1).  scripts/claims.sh gained the gate kind: an
      8-segment path is accepted when segment 5 is gate, segment 7 is
      validated as 12 lowercase hex and carried in the projection as
      rev=, and segments 6 and 8 are project and gate-slug.  The wrapper
      sanitizes "/" and other path separators in project and gate-slug
      to "-" so the path stays 8 segments.  It writes the observation
      and skip-allowed list as tree values first, so claims.sh's
      input-published check passes, then mints the run.

      MEASURED (2026-11-24): a receipt minted by root verified under
      claims.sh verify for a fresh non-admin identity, and that identity
      received 404 on the runner's run row - the citation alone carried
      it.  The before/after differential replay is recorded in the
      verify lines below.

      CONTENT ADDRESSING, and what the tamper test can say.  Changing
      any observation field changes the observation tree, so its hash
      and the citation hash change: the changed facts are a different
      receipt, never an edit of the original.  The original citation
      still verifies against the facts it pins, because values are
      immutable.  A citation that names the changed input under the
      original result fails verification exactly when the verdict
      changes (measured: exit 0 to 1 turns pass into fail; the tampered
      citation reported MISMATCH, exit 1).  claims.sh verify takes a
      citation, not a candidate observation, so "verify the original
      path with changed facts" is not a shape the machinery has: the
      binding is the hash, not a re-derivation of the observation.

      Because the run is prim-free, the claims.sh VERIFIABLE property
      holds: any identity resolves the cited program and inputs by
      hash, re-executes under the same caps, and compares result hash
      and step count, with no access to the runner's run row or
      journal. A sealed derivation record (deriv.borg) is available for
      offline checking with deriv-check.

      What a verified receipt PROVES: this verdict follows from this
      exact observation record under this exact policy, and identity R
      asserted the record. What it does NOT prove: that the command ran
      or exited as recorded, or that the tests test the right thing.
      Those stay with REPRODUCTION (a reviewer re-runs the command at
      rev and compares exit and counts) and with independent review.
      Attestation is why the runner identity matters: a receipt minted
      by the implementer's own identity is weaker evidence than one
      minted by a separate factory runner identity, and review-change
      should say which it got.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection integration
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Seams that exist today (no new subsystem):
        - nomia verify-change: its report gains an optional receipt
          (claim path) per command; review-change's check-evidence.sh
          gains a mode that resolves each receipt with claims.sh verify
          and fails when a receipt's verdict disagrees with the claimed
          exit or pass.
        - factory/evidence.py and the loop's gate runs: emit one receipt
          per gate command; a card cannot advance on a TEST gate whose
          receipt verdict is unverified (a build's receipt is recorded
          and its own exit decides).
        - tuna's own make test: the first runner, via a planned wrapper
          scripts/gate-receipt.sh, so tuna dogfoods its own gate.

      ORDER. G1 LANDED (2026-11-24): observation encoding, verdict
      program, the harness adapters (alcotest, differential, and the
      make-test aggregate) and the wrapper, against tuna's own suite.
      G2 LANDED (2026-11-27): check-evidence.sh receipt mode. G3
      LANDED (2026-11-27): the factory loop emits receipts for every
      gate, law for the test gate. Each step stands alone; G1 is
      useful with no factory change.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection g2-receipt-check
   (status implemented)
   (execution
    (scope g2)
    (kind code))
   (details
    (doc (|
      G2 of gate-receipts/integration. nomia's
      skills/review-change/scripts/check-evidence.sh
      (nomia/skills/review-change/scripts/check-evidence.sh is the
      reviewed source) gains a `--receipts` mode: a command's evidence
      may carry a `"receipt"` field naming the claim path
      gate-receipt.sh minted for it, and the mode resolves that receipt
      with claims.sh verify and fails when the receipt's verdict
      disagrees with the command's claimed exit or pass. G1's receipt is
      the verdict run's claim, so its verdict is that run's result tree
      (pass 10, fail 110, unverified 1110); claims.sh verify therefore
      prints verdict=<pass|fail|unverified> beside VERIFIED/MISMATCH for
      a claim whose citation carries kind=gate, mapped from the
      reproduced result tree, and prints no verdict line for any other
      claim, so an ordinary claim's output is unchanged (a non-gate
      result that happens to be the tree 10, 110 or 1110 gets no verdict
      line). An unreproducible (MISMATCH) or unresolvable receipt,
      or a reproduced unverified verdict against a claimed pass, fails
      the check - never "unchecked but fine". Without `--receipts` the
      existing completeness check is unchanged, and the mode is offline
      except for the claims.sh verify call a report explicitly asks for.
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "G2: check-evidence.sh --receipts resolves each command's `receipt` claim with claims.sh verify (stubbed in the offline test): a pass receipt with exit 0 and a fail receipt with exit 1 agree and pass; a pass receipt with exit 1 or a fail receipt with exit 0 disagrees and fails; a MISMATCH, an unresolvable receipt, or a reproduced unverified receipt with exit 0 (claimed pass) fails, while a command without a `receipt` is not a receipt failure; without the mode the existing completeness fixtures behave unchanged"))
  )
  (subsection g3-factory-receipts
   (status implemented)
   (execution
    (scope g3)
    (kind code))
   (details
    (doc (|
      G3 of gate-receipts/integration. The loop's card-advancing TEST
      gates - the implement pass (factory/loop.py:4400) and the
      merge pass (factory/loop.py:5295) - each run one gate command
      under tuna/scripts/gate-receipt.sh with that command's captured
      log, and factory/evidence.py receives the mint's claim path and
      verdict and records them per command as {label, cmd, rc, receipt,
      verdict}. The BUILD command of each pass is recorded the same way
      but is not a receipt-law gate: a successful build prints no test
      summary, so its receipt is unverified by construction and would
      block every card (measured 2026-11-27: `dune build` exit 0 ->
      counts unparsed -> unverified). A green build advances on its own
      exit; a red build still blocks. When the wrapper cannot mint at
      all, the build runs directly and the record says so, so a missing
      runner never skips compilation. The gate's own result is the command's exit. When the
      wrapper minted, that exit is read from its `exit=` line:
      gate-receipt.sh exits 0 whenever a receipt was minted whatever the
      verdict and 2 on operational failure
      (tuna/scripts/gate-receipt.sh:36-38), so its process exit is never
      the gate result. When it did not, the exit is the direct run's (see
      the build fallback above). A gate that does not apply
      (gate_applies false, no dune project) is recorded not-run, never as
      a missing receipt. The uifix image gate (factory/loop.py:4755) and
      the rollback revert gate (factory/loop.py:4873) are not G3's
      card-advancing gates: they keep their existing verdicts (uifix
      SKIP/FAIL, rollback RED/CONFLICT) and the receipt law does not
      touch them. The runner identity comes from the factory's receipt
      configuration in factory/config.json - a base URL and a token
      file, never a token value - and the wrapper's env carries
      TUNA_BASE and TUNA_TOKEN past dune_env's allowlist
      (factory/loop.py:2724). A card cannot advance on a TEST gate whose
      receipt verdict is unverified: an unconfigured runner, a mint
      failure (including a log above the value cap, exit 2), a
      resolution failure, a missing receipt, or a pass TEST gate with a
      non-pass receipt blocks the pass with a diagnostic that names the
      gate - never a skip and never a silent green. A build advances on
      its own exit; its receipt is recorded, never law. Blocking is an
      abort, not a log line: the implement pass records a BLOCKED ledger
      row, sets no code_pass_state and keeps its branch, because a
      config or mint failure is not a diff failure; the merge returns
      before the push and leaves master untouched. A red gate's receipt
      may read fail; its redness comes from its own exit and it already
      blocks. The gate slug passed to the wrapper is unique per
      invocation (it carries the pass and the attempt), because
      claims.sh is write-once and refuses an existing
      u.<runner>.claim.<date>.gate.<project>.<rev12>.<slug> path
      (tuna/scripts/claims.sh:203-207); re-running a gate whose receipt
      already exists resolves that claim instead of failing. Resolution
      is claims.sh verify's exit status only; the verdict recorded is the
      one gate-receipt.sh printed at mint (line 253), so G3 changes
      neither the wrapper nor claims.sh and does not depend on G2's
      verdict= line. The seam takes an injected receipt runner so the law
      is testable offline with a stub that answers pass, fail or
      unverified without a network; with no injection the production
      default is the real wrapper, and an absent configuration blocks
      rather than switching receipts off.
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "G3: every card-advancing test gate command the loop runs (the implement and merge test commands) produces one receipt (claim path and verdict) recorded per command in the evidence the ledger row carries and the advance check reads, and the build command is recorded the same way but is not a receipt-law gate; a pass TEST gate whose receipt verdict is not pass, a missing receipt, an unresolvable receipt, a mint that failed (including a log above the value cap), or an unconfigured base/token file blocks the pass with a diagnostic naming the gate - the merge leaves master untouched, and the implement pass reads the same decision and names the test gate (its BLOCKED ledger row and kept branch are code reading, not an executed test) - while a green build with an unverified receipt advances and a red build still blocks on its own exit; a gate that does not apply is recorded not-run, not as a missing receipt; the rollback and uifix gates keep their existing verdicts and are not receipt-law gates; a minted gate's rc is read from the wrapper's exit= line, not the wrapper's process exit, and an unmintable build's rc is the direct run's; and an injected runner answering pass leaves existing gate behavior unchanged (factory/tests/test_evidence.py for the receipt-verdict decision, factory/tests/test_merge_gates.py for the loop's implement and merge refusal, the green-build-unverified advance and the red-build block in a temp repo with /bin/true gates; the unconfigured case is exercised with no injection, not with a stub)"))
  )
  (subsection failure-definitions
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Defined in advance, per acceptance.borg's regime; record any
      observed failure in FINDINGS.md with evidence:
        GF1  a reproduced fail-open gate (exit 0, nothing checked) still
             yields pass: the policy or adapter is wrong.
        GF2  verifying a receipt costs a reviewer more than re-running
             the check: the format is not paying for itself.
        GF3  after G3, factory reviewers still cite exit codes and not
             receipts: no one uses it, and the chapter is refuted on
             adoption.
        GF4  the verdict needs a prim to decide a real gate: the
             prim-free property, and with it third-party verification,
             does not survive contact with real gates.
    |))
   )
  )
 )
 (verify
  (test "verdict fixtures: an observation with exit 0 and counts [0 0 0 0] is unverified; exit 0 with [15 15 0 15] (the pp SKIP shape) is unverified unless the gate is skip-allowed; exit 0 with [93 93 0 0] is pass; exit 1 is fail; unparsed counts are unverified")
  (test "replaying the 2026-11-23 differential failure (tuna at a90487b3, before 3cd448d5) through scripts/gate-receipt.sh yields fail, because the log's 93/93 FAILED outranks its misleading sbcl-missing sentence, and the same gate at 3cd448d5 yields pass")
  (test "a receipt minted by one identity verifies under claims.sh verify for a second, non-admin identity with no access to the runner's run row")
  (test "changing any observation field changes the cited input hash, so the changed facts are a different citation (a different claim path), never a silent edit of the original; a citation naming the changed input under the original result fails verification when the verdict differs")
  (test "check-evidence.sh in receipt mode fails a report that claims pass for a command whose receipt verdict is unverified")))
